# `navigationDestination(item:)` — clear binding on appear

When a screen **owns** a nested push via `navigationDestination(item:)` (Item → Outfit, Outfit → Item, Pending Redress → Edit OutfitAddView):

## Do

- Clear the destination binding in the **pushed** view’s `onAppear` so SwiftUI does not keep re-evaluating the destination builder (that loop freezes the UI).
- Keep `.id(...)` on the pushed root when the session/identity must stay stable.
- Use the child’s `dismiss()` (or system back) to pop; do not rely on the binding staying non-nil to keep the screen on the stack.

```swift
.navigationDestination(item: $editDestination) { destination in
    ChildView(...)
        .id(destination.id)
        .onAppear {
            // Critical: prevent destination from being re-evaluated in a loop.
            editDestination = nil
        }
}
```

## References

- `ItemDetailView` → `OutfitAddView` / paired `ItemDetailView` / `OutfitDetailView`
- `OutfitDetailView` → `ItemDetailView`
- `PendingOutfitDetailView` → Edit `OutfitAddView` (`editRedressDestination`)

## Don’t confuse with Profile root ownership

When **Profile** (or another root) owns the binding that represents the *currently visible* pushed screen (e.g. grid → item detail), do **not** nil that binding from the child’s `onAppear` — that can orphan the parent push. See `profile-nested-navigation.mdc`.

Also **do not** clear on appear for Friends / Users / Notifications → other-user `ProfileView` — that orphans the push and breaks Back (`< Friends`). Those mid-stack profile pushes keep the binding until system pop (same as Profile root ownership).

| Owner of binding | Clear on child’s `onAppear`? |
|---|---|
| Mid-stack screen pushing Item / Outfit / Pending / Edit | **Yes** — required to avoid freeze |
| Friends / Users / Notifications → other-user Profile | **No** — keep until system pop |
| Profile/root binding for the active pushed child | **No** — keep until system pop |
